<<<
supervacaneous. A SUPERFLUOUS WORD. .
===
**supervacaneous.** A ^^SUPERFLUOUS WORD^^.
>>>

<<<
supervise, not -vize; see -ISE.
===
**supervise,** not *-vize*; see ^^-ISE^^.
>>>

<<<
supine. The grammatical noun (see TECHNICAL TERMS) is pronounced sii’pin.
===
**supine.** The grammatical noun (see ^^TECHNICAL TERMS^^) is pronounced sū'pīn.
>>>

<<<
pronunciation is stipi’n, & of six verse quotations
===
pronunciation is sūpī'n, & of six verse quotations
>>>

<<<
which st’pin is natural, though not quite necessary. But RECESSIVE ACCENT is likely to prevail sooner or later, as in CANINE &c.
===
which sū'pīn is natural, though not quite necessary. But ^^RECESSIVE ACCENT^^ is likely to prevail sooner or later, as in ^^CANINE^^ &c.
>>>

<<<
supple. The fine mass of the head, solidly yet supplely modelled, is set in a particularly beautiful convention of the hair. The adverb is supply, not supplely; cf. susTLE.
===
**supple.** *The fine mass of the head, solidly yet* supplely *modelled, is set in a particularly beautiful convention of the hair.* The adverb is *supply,* not *supplely*; cf. ^^SUBTLE^^.
>>>

<<<
It is true that the OED has found more instances in print of -plely than of -ply, & therefore on its historical principles makes supplely the standard form.
===
It is true that the OED has found more instances in print of *-plely* than of *-ply*, & therefore on its historical principles makes *supplely* the standard form.
>>>

<<<
But the pronunciation is undoubtedly st’pli, not si’pzl-li, & the long spelling has been due to the wish to distinguish to the eye from supply (supli’) n. & vb; such devices
===
But the pronunciation is undoubtedly sŭ'plĭ, not sŭ'p*u*l-lĭ, & the long spelling has been due to the wish to distinguish to the eye from *supply* (s*u*plī') n. & vb; such devices
>>>

<<<
are not legitimate except in the last necessity, as with singeing & singing; & it is to be observed that, whereas the -e- in singeing selects the right of two possible
===
are not legitimate except in the last necessity, as with *singeing* & *singing*; & it is to be observed that, whereas the -e- in *singeing* selects the right of two possible
>>>

<<<
pronunciations, the -le- in supplely suggests a wrong one.
===
pronunciations, the -le- in *supplely* suggests a wrong one.
>>>

<<<
tion; subtly is in fact the best analogue, & its spelling, though subtlely has been occasionally used, is now established.
===
tion; *subtly* is in fact the best analogue, & its spelling, though *subtlely* has been occasionally used, is now established.
>>>

<<<
supplement. Noun su’pliment; verb suiplimé’nt or sti’plimént; see Noun & VERB ACCENT C. |
===
**supplement.** Noun sŭ'plĭm*e*nt; verb sŭplĭmĕ'nt or sŭ'plĭmĕnt; see ^^NOUN & VERB ACCENT^^ C.
>>>

<<<
supply, vb. For inflexions see VERBS IN -IE &c., 6.

587

supposal.
===
**supply,** vb. For inflexions see ^^VERBS IN -IE^^ &c., 6.

supposal.
>>>

<<<
supposal. See -AL NOUNS.
===
**supposal.** See ^^-AL NOUNS^^.
>>>

<<<
supposedly. Four syllables if used; see -EDLY.
===
**supposedly.** Four syllables if used; see ^^-EDLY^^.
>>>

<<<
suppositious, supposititious. The supposititious elector who imagined that the Parliament Bill was a weapon for show & not for use is, we venture to say, a mythical being.
===
**suppositious, supposititious.** *The supposititious elector who imagined that the Parliament Bill was a weapon for show & not for use is, we venture to say, a mythical being.*
>>>

<<<
like pacifist by the side of pacificist (see -Ist A).
===
like *pacifist* by the side of *pacificist* (see ^^-IST A^^).
>>>

<<<
pronounce supposititious; but there is no reason to call it wrong; suppositious & suppositilious may as well coexist, if there is work for two words, as factious & factitious;
===
pronounce *supposititious*; but there is no reason to call it wrong; *suppositious* & *supposititious* may as well coexist, if there is work for two words, as *factious* & *factitious*;
>>>

<<<
there are ambitious, expeditious, seditious, nutritious, cautious, & oblivious, to supply it.
===
there are *ambitious, expeditious, seditious, nutritious, cautious,* & *oblivious,* to supply it.
>>>

<<<
viz spurious, & hypothetical. Supposititious is directly from the Latin p.p. suppositus= substituted or put
===
viz spurious, & hypothetical. *Supposititious* is directly from the Latin p.p. *suppositus*= substituted or put
>>>

<<<
ostensible. Swppositious is from the English supposition= hypothesis (cf. suspicious similarly formed
===
ostensible. *Suppositious* is from the English *supposition*= hypothesis (cf. *suspicious* similarly formed
>>>

<<<
It does not follow that suppositious is wanted; probably
===
It does not follow that *suppositious* is wanted; probably
>>>

<<<
it does follow that supposititious should not be given,
===
it does follow that *supposititious* should not be given,
>>>

<<<
senses proper to the synonyms of suppositious, but should be confined
===
senses proper to the synonyms of *suppositious*, but should be confined
>>>

<<<
suppress makes suppressible, see -ABLE 2; & suppressor, see -OR.
===
**suppress** makes *suppressible*, see ^^-ABLE^^ 2; & *suppressor*, see ^^-OR^^.
>>>

<<<
suppressedly. A bad form; -EDLY.
===
**suppressedly.** A bad form; ^^-EDLY^^.
>>>

<<<
suppressio veri. See TECHNICAL TERMS. Pronounce -ér’-i. supreme. See POSITIVE WORDS, &, for the s. sacrifice, STOCK PATHOS.
===
**suppressio veri.** See ^^TECHNICAL TERMS^^. Pronounce -e͞r'-ī.

**supreme.** See ^^POSITIVE WORDS^^, &, for *the s. sacrifice*, ^^STOCK PATHOS^^.
>>>
